crates/true-peak-limiter/tests/mono_collapse.rs:1://! The mono-collapse body renders the dual body's left plane, and the disengage copy is complete.
crates/true-peak-limiter/tests/mono_collapse.rs-2-//!
crates/true-peak-limiter/tests/mono_collapse.rs:3://! The crate-level statement of what the strip's console gates check end to end, and it is here as
crates/true-peak-limiter/tests/mono_collapse.rs-4-//! well as there for one reason: the console fixture carries no automation, so **no ramp is ever in
crates/true-peak-limiter/tests/mono_collapse.rs:5://! flight while a track is collapse-eligible** -- every parameter write in this engine is addressed
crates/true-peak-limiter/tests/mono_collapse.rs-6-//! to one channel and therefore clears the witness' `LIVE` term on the block it lands. That makes
crates/true-peak-limiter/tests/mono_collapse.rs-7-//! the ramp-carrying entries of this effect's disengage copy list unreachable from the engine, and
crates/true-peak-limiter/tests/mono_collapse.rs-8-//! reachable from here, where the contract call is made directly.
crates/true-peak-limiter/tests/mono_collapse.rs-9-//!
crates/true-peak-limiter/tests/mono_collapse.rs-10-//! The content is deliberately **loud** and the ceiling deliberately low: a signal the limiter
crates/true-peak-limiter/tests/mono_collapse.rs-11-//! never reduces leaves `required_ring` at `1.0` and makes the twelve oversampling history taps
crates/true-peak-limiter/tests/mono_collapse.rs:12://! unobservable, so a corpus below the ceiling would say nothing about half of this kernel's state.
